-- title:
--   An infeasible integer system has an integral Farkas certificate with $\|y\|_1 \le (n+1)(n+1)!U^{n+1}$
-- statement:
--   **An infeasible integer system has an integral Farkas certificate of bounded $\ell^1$ norm.**
--
--   Let $Ax \ge b$ be an integer system in $n \ge 1$ variables, every entry bounded by $U \ge 1$ in absolute value, and suppose it has no real solution. Then there is an **integer** vector $y \ge 0$ with
--   $$y^{\mathsf T}A = 0, \qquad y^{\mathsf T}b \ge 1, \qquad \sum_i y_i \;\le\; (n+1)\,(n+1)!\,U^{\,n+1}.$$
--
--   **Why each clause.** Farkas' lemma — already `Proved` on this platform as `LinearOptimization.farkas_lemma` — supplies a *real* $y \ge 0$ with $y^{\mathsf T}A = 0$ and $y^{\mathsf T}b > 0$. That is not enough for Khachiyan's argument, which needs the certificate to be *quantitatively* bounded: the perturbation $\varepsilon = 1/(2(n+1)(n+1)!U^{n+1})$ is calibrated against exactly this $\ell^1$ bound, and against nothing else.
--
--   The two upgrades are:
--
--   1. **Integrality, hence $y^{\mathsf T}b \ge 1$.** A positive real number is not bounded below; a positive *integer* is. Scaling the certificate to clear denominators turns $y^{\mathsf T}b > 0$ into $y^{\mathsf T}b \ge 1$, and this is the only place the gap between "positive" and "at least one" is bought.
--
--   2. **The $\ell^1$ bound.** This is the mathematical content and it is not formal. One must first replace the certificate by a *basic* solution of the cone $\{y \ge 0,\ y^{\mathsf T}A = 0,\ y^{\mathsf T}b = 1\}$, whose support therefore has at most $n+1$ elements; its nonzero entries are then given by Cramer's rule applied to a nonsingular square subsystem of $[A \mid b]^{\mathsf T}$, whose data are integers bounded by $U$. Clearing the common denominator (a determinant) leaves entries that are themselves determinants of $(n+1)\times(n+1)$ integer matrices with entries bounded by $U$, so each is at most $(n+1)!\,U^{\,n+1}$ by the permutation-expansion bound, and there are at most $n+1$ of them.
--
--   **What the platform already provides.** The determinant estimates are done: `SmaleNinth.abs_det_le_factorial_mul_pow` and `SmaleNinth.cramer_solution_bound` are both `Proved`, as is `LinearOptimization.farkas_lemma`. What is *missing* — in Mathlib as well as here — is the existence of a basic solution: that a nonempty polyhedron whose lineality space is trivial has a basic feasible solution, equivalently that a pointed cone is generated by its extreme rays. Mathlib has `Polytope`/`extremePoints` material but nothing that produces a basic solution of an explicit inequality system with the support bound. That gap is the real work here, and it is why this is a separate statement rather than an inlined step.
--
--   **The constant is deliberately generous.** The sharper bound $(n+1)\,n!\,U^{\,n}$ also holds and would give $\varepsilon\|y\|_1 \le 1/(2(n+1)U)$. The bound stated here yields $\varepsilon\|y\|_1 \le 1/2$, which is all the parent needs, and it is the bound that falls straight out of the permutation expansion without a further refinement of the submatrix size. Anyone proving the sharper version should feel free to strengthen the statement.
--
--   **Where it is used.** This is the sole remaining gap in `SmaleNinth.khachiyan_nonempty_imp`, the Farkas direction of the Khachiyan feasibility equivalence: given a point $x$ of the relaxed-and-boxed system, $0 = y^{\mathsf T}(Ax) \ge y^{\mathsf T}b - \varepsilon\|y\|_1 \ge 1 - \tfrac12 > 0$, a contradiction. That assembly is proved outright.
-- source:
--   L. G. Khachiyan, A polynomial algorithm in linear programming, Soviet Math. Doklady 20 (1979) 191-194; B. Korte and J. Vygen, Combinatorial Optimization, 6th ed., Springer, sections 4.4-4.5; D. Bertsimas and J. N. Tsitsiklis, Introduction to Linear Optimization, Athena Scientific 1997, section 8.4 and Theorem 2.4 (basic feasible solutions).

